'''Myocarditis''' is [[inflammation]] of the [[cardiac muscle]]. Myocarditis can progress to '''inflammatory cardiomyopathy''' when there is associated ventricular remodeling and cardiac dysfunction due to chronic inflammation.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Suresh |first1=Abhilash |last2=Martens |first2=Pieter |last3=Tang |first3=W. H. Wilson |date=October 2022 |title=Biomarkers for Myocarditis and Inflammatory Cardiomyopathy |journal=Current Heart Failure Reports |language=en |volume=19 |issue=5 |pages=346–355 |doi=10.1007/s11897-022-00569-8 |pmid=35913661 |issn=1546-9530|pmc=9340754 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Tschöpe |first1=Carsten |last2=Ammirati |first2=Enrico |last3=Bozkurt |first3=Biykem |last4=Caforio |first4=Alida L. P. |last5=Cooper |first5=Leslie T. |last6=Felix |first6=Stephan B. |last7=Hare |first7=Joshua M. |last8=Heidecker |first8=Bettina |last9=Heymans |first9=Stephane |last10=Hübner |first10=Norbert |last11=Kelle |first11=Sebastian |last12=Klingel |first12=Karin |last13=Maatz |first13=Henrike |last14=Parwani |first14=Abdul S. |last15=Spillmann |first15=Frank |date=March 2021 |title=Myocarditis and inflammatory cardiomyopathy: current evidence and future directions |journal=Nature Reviews Cardiology |language=en |volume=18 |issue=3 |pages=169–193 |doi=10.1038/s41569-020-00435-x |pmid=33046850 |issn=1759-5002|pmc=7548534 }}</ref> Symptoms can include [[shortness of breath]], [[chest pain]], [[Exercise intolerance|decreased ability to exercise]], and an [[irregular heartbeat]].<ref name=NEJM2009/> The duration of problems can vary from hours to months. Complications may include [[heart failure]], due to [[dilated cardiomyopathy]] or [[cardiac arrest]].<ref name="NEJM2009">{{Cite journal |vauthors=Cooper LT |date=April 2009 |title=Myocarditis |journal=The New England Journal of Medicine |volume=360 |issue=15 |pages=1526–1538 |doi=10.1056/nejmra0800028 |pmc=5814110 |pmid=19357408}}</ref>

<!-- Cause and diagnosis-->
Myocarditis is most often due to a [[viral infection]].<ref name=NEJM2009/> Other causes include [[bacterial infections]], certain medications, toxins and [[autoimmune disorders]].<ref name=NEJM2009/><ref name=Kin2012/> A diagnosis may be supported by an [[electrocardiogram]] (ECG), increased [[troponin]], [[cardiac magnetic resonance imaging|heart MRI]], and occasionally a heart [[biopsy]].<ref name=NEJM2009/><ref name="Kin2012">{{Cite journal |display-authors=6 |vauthors=Kindermann I, Barth C, Mahfoud F, Ukena C, Lenski M, Yilmaz A, Klingel K, Kandolf R, Sechtem U, Cooper LT, Böhm M |date=February 2012 |title=Update on myocarditis |journal=Journal of the American College of Cardiology |volume=59 |issue=9 |pages=779–792 |doi=10.1016/j.jacc.2011.09.074 |pmid=22361396 |doi-access=free}}</ref> An [[echocardiogram|ultrasound of the heart]] is important to rule out other potential causes, such as [[valvular heart disease|heart valve problems]].<ref name=Kin2012/>

Treatment depends on both the severity and the cause.<ref name=NEJM2009/><ref name=Kin2012/> Medications such as [[ACE inhibitors]], [[beta blockers]], and [[diuretics]] are often used.<ref name=NEJM2009/><ref name=Kin2012/> A period of no exercise is typically recommended during recovery.<ref name=NEJM2009/><ref name=Kin2012/> [[Corticosteroids]] or [[intravenous immunoglobulin]] (IVIG) may be useful in certain cases.<ref name=NEJM2009/><ref name=Kin2012/> In severe cases, an [[implantable cardiac defibrillator]] or [[heart transplant]] may be recommended.<ref name=NEJM2009/><ref name=Kin2012/>

In 2013, about 1.5 million cases of acute myocarditis occurred.<ref>{{Cite journal |vauthors=Vos T, Barber RM, Bell B, Bertozzi-Villa A, Biryukov S, Bolliger I, etal |date=August 2015 |title=Global, regional, and national incidence, prevalence, and years lived with disability for 301 acute and chronic diseases and injuries in 188 countries, 1990–2013: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2013 |journal=Lancet |volume=386 |issue=9995 |pages=743–800 |doi=10.1016/s0140-6736(15)60692-4 |pmc=4561509 |pmid=26063472 |collaboration=Global Burden of Disease Study 2013 Collaborators}}</ref> While people of all ages are affected, the young are most often affected.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-nIpAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA135 |title=Cellular and Molecular Pathobiology of Cardiovascular Disease |vauthors=Willis M, Homeister JW, Stone JR |date=2013 |publisher=Academic Press |isbn=978-0-12-405525-4 |page=135 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171105195029/https://books.google.com/books?id=-nIpAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA135 |archive-date=2017-11-05 |url-status=live}}</ref> It is slightly more common in males than females.<ref name=NEJM2009/> Most cases are mild.<ref name=Kin2012/> In 2015, [[cardiomyopathy]], including myocarditis, resulted in 354,000 deaths, up from 294,000 in 1990.<ref name="GDB2013">{{Cite journal |vauthors=Naghavi M, Wang H, Lozano R, Davis A, Liang X, Zhou M, etal |date=January 2015 |title=Global, regional, and national age-sex specific all-cause and cause-specific mortality for 240 causes of death, 1990–2013: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2013 |journal=Lancet |volume=385 |issue=9963 |pages=117–171 |doi=10.1016/S0140-6736(14)61682-2 |pmc=4340604 |pmid=25530442 |collaboration=GBD 2013 Mortality and Causes of Death Collaborators}}</ref><ref name="GBD2015De" /> The initial descriptions of the condition are from the mid-1800s.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=xlQ1hqp9ngwC&pg=PA263 |title=Infectious Diseases in Critical Care Medicine |vauthors=Cunha BA |date=2009 |publisher=CRC Press |isbn=978-1-4200-1960-5 |page=263 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171105195029/https://books.google.com/books?id=xlQ1hqp9ngwC&pg=PA263 |archive-date=2017-11-05 |url-status=live}}</ref>

== Signs and symptoms ==
The signs and symptoms associated with myocarditis are varied, and relate either to the actual inflammation of the [[myocardium]] or to the weakness and dysfunction of the heart muscle that is secondary to the inflammation. While myocarditis may develop over periods ranging from hours to months, patients typically present with signs and symptoms that resemble heart failure, including the following:<ref name="NEJM2009" /><ref name=Feldman>{{cite journal | vauthors = Feldman AM, McNamara D | title = Myocarditis | journal = The New England Journal of Medicine | volume = 343 | issue = 19 | pages = 1388–98 | date = November 2000 | pmid = 11070105 | doi = 10.1056/NEJM200011093431908 }}</ref>
